 \section[One b-jet signal region (SR1b) optimisation and definition]{One b-jet signal region ($\SR$) optimisation and definition}
 \label{sec:ch7_sr1b}
 
 The $\SR$ regions are defined by requiring exactly one $b$-tagged jet to target the $b\tau$ interaction in the $U_1$ model and suppress inclusive $W$+jets production.
 The SM subprocesses $gu\to b\tau\nu$ and $gc\to b\tau\nu$ are suppressed by the small CKM elements $V_{ub}$ and $V_{cb}$, respectively~\cite{Endo2022NonResonant}.
-Competing SM $\tau\nu+\mathrm{jets}$ events without a genuine $b$-jet enter this category through mistagging.
+Competing SM $\tau\nu+\mathrm{jets}$ events without a $b$-jet are selected in $\SR$ through mistagging.
 
 The $b$-jet $\pT$ is used to distinguish resonant $U_1\to b\tau$ decays, which produce an energetic decay jet, from non-resonant production, where the radiated jet is typically softer.
 The sample is divided at $\pT^b=250~\GeV$, with $\pT^b>250~\GeV$ defining $\SR$-$\Res$ and $50<\pT^b<250~\GeV$ defining $\SR$-$\NonRes$.
